      "text": "We shipped a feature nobody used.\n\nThe team had spent weeks designing it, building it, polishing edge cases, and preparing the launch. Internally, everyone agreed it was valuable.\n\nCustomers disagreed—mostly by ignoring it.\n\nAt first, we blamed discoverability. We changed the placement, added tooltips, and mentioned it in onboarding.\n\nUsage barely moved.\n\nThe uncomfortable truth: we had validated the problem, but not the urgency.\n\nCustomers agreed the feature sounded useful. But “useful” wasn’t enough to change their behavior. The problem wasn’t frequent, painful, or important enough to earn a place in their workflow.\n\nThat experience changed how I think about product development.\n\nBefore investing heavily, I now try to answer:\n\n- When did the customer last experience this problem?\n- What are they doing today to solve it?\n- What does that workaround cost them?\n- Would they change their behavior—or pay—to make the problem disappear?\n- What is the smallest experiment that could test real demand?\n\nPositive feedback can be misleading. People are generous with opinions, especially when saying yes costs nothing.\n\nBehavior is stronger evidence.\n\nA waitlist is stronger than enthusiasm.  \nA prototype test is stronger than a survey.  \nA manual pilot is stronger than a feature request.  \nRepeated use is stronger than all of them.\n\nThe feature wasn’t a complete failure. It gave us something more valuable than adoption: a better standard for deciding what to build.\n\nDon’t just validate that a problem exists.\n\nValidate that it matters enough for someone to act.\n\n#ProductManagement #ProductDevelopment #Startups #CustomerDiscovery #LessonsLearned"
     }
